 IdType="pubmed">24882624</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Glebov-McCloud A. G. P., Saide W. S., Gaine M. E., Strack S., Protein Kinase A in neurological disorders. J. Neurodev. Disord. 16, 9 (2024).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC10936040</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">38481146</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Marbach F., et al. , Variants in PRKAR1B cause a neurodevelopmental disorder with autism spectrum disorder, apraxia, and insensitivity to pain. Genet. Med. 23, 1465&#x2013;1473 (2021).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC8354857</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">33833410</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sears J. C., Choi W. J., Broadie K., Fragile X Mental Retardation Protein positively regulates PKA anchor Rugose and PKA activity to control actin assembly in learning/memory circuitry. Neurobiol. Dis. 127, 53&#x2013;64 (2019).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6588493</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30771457</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Jiang A., et al. , Sex differences in dopamine receptor signaling in Fmr1 knockout mice: A pilot study. Brain Sciences 11, 1398 (2021).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC8615700</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">34827397</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Pinto M. J., Pedro J. R., Costa R. O., Almeida R. D., Visualizing K48 ubiquitination during presynaptic formation by ubiquitination-induced fluorescence complementation (UiFC). Front. Molec. Neurosci. 9, 43 (2016).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4901079</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">27375430</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Poddar R., Deb I., Mukherjee S., Paul S., NR2B-NMDA receptor mediated modulation of the tyrosine phosphatase STEP regulates glutamates induced neuronal cell death. J. Neurochem. 115, 1350&#x2013;1362 (2010).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3035421</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21029094</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Dunkley P. R., Jarvie P. E., Robinson P. J., A rapid Percoll gradient procedure for preparation of synaptosomes. Nat. Protoc. 3, 1718&#x2013;1728 (2008).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18927557</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">42214628</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>11</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>11</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1879-2596</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>1873</Volume><Issue>6</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Aug</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Biochimica et biophysica acta. Molecular cell research</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Biochim Biophys Acta Mol Cell Res</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>RREB1-driven FMR1 upregulation promotes colorectal cancer angiogenesis via VEGFA-mediated MAPK/PI3K signaling activation.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>120165</StartPage><MedlinePgn>120165</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bbamcr.2026.120165</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0167-4889(26)00063-7</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Colorectal cancer (CRC) remains a leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide, and angiogenesis is a critical driver of tumor growth and metastasis. In this study, we found that FMR1, an RNA-binding protein, was markedly upregulated in CRC tissues and was positively associated with microvessel density (MVD), CD34 expression, and VEGFA levels. Functional experiments, including HUVEC tube formation and chick chorioallantoic membrane (CAM) assays, showed that FMR1 overexpression promoted angiogenesis, whereas FMR1 knockdown suppressed this process. Mechanistically, FMR1 enhanced VEGFA expression and secretion, thereby activating MAPK and PI3K signaling pathways in endothelial cells and promoting their proliferation and tube formation. Notably, VEGFA silencing attenuated the pro-angiogenic effects induced by FMR1. In addition, we identified RREB1 as an upstream transcriptional activator of FMR1. ChIP-qPCR and luciferase reporter assays demonstrated that RREB1 directly binds to the FMR1 promoter, and RREB1 expression was positively correlated with FMR1 levels in CRC tissues. Collectively, these findings reveal a previously unrecognized RREB1-FMR1-VEGFA signaling axis that promotes angiogenesis in CRC, highlighting its potential as a prognostic biomarker and therapeutic target.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hu</LastName><ForeName>Yuhan</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Pathology, School of Basic Medical Sciences, Henan Medical University, Xinxiang, Henan, 453000, China; Micromorphology Laboratory, School of Basic Medical Sciences, Henan Medical University, Xinxiang, Henan, 453000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yu</LastName><ForeName>Pei</ForeName><Initials>P</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Pathology, School of Basic Medical Sciences, Henan Medical University, Xinxiang, Henan, 453000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yan</LastName><ForeName>Zhaoxiang</ForeName><Initials>Z</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Pathology, School of Basic Medical Sciences, Henan Medical University, Xinxiang, Henan, 453000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastNam